.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
Unsetting a trap is different from setting an empty trap command.
\f[CR]bc_state_set_trap(c, \(dqSIGINT\(dq, \(dq\(dq)\f[R] causes the
signal to be ignored, while
\f[CR]bc_state_unset_trap(c, \(dqSIGINT\(dq)\f[R] restores the default
signal handler.
This matches the distinction between \f[CR]trap \(aq\(aq SIGINT\f[R] and
\f[CR]trap \- SIGINT\f[R] in bash.
.IP \(bu 2
Unsetting a trap for a signal that was never trapped is not an error.
The signal remains at its default disposition.
.IP \(bu 2
The EXIT pseudo\-signal trap is typically set for cleanup operations.
Unsetting it before session termination prevents the cleanup code from
running.
.IP \(bu 2
Unsetting the DEBUG trap restores normal command execution without
per\-command hook invocations.
.IP \(bu 2
The function uses the v2 binary protocol internally.
The STATE channel request includes \f[CR]\(dqop\(dq: \(dqunset\(dq\f[R],
\f[CR]\(dqns\(dq: \(dqtrap\(dq\f[R], and \f[CR]\(dqname\(dq\f[R] (signal
name) fields.
.IP \(bu 2
A single \f[CR]bc_client_t\f[R] must not be used concurrently from
multiple threads.
